    "gold_solution": "```python\nimport math\nfrom gurobipy import *\n\ndef solve_problem():\n    m = Model(\"VRPTW\")\n    m.setParam('TimeLimit', 300)\n\n    # --- Data ---\n    n_customers = 5\n    n_vehicles = 3\n    Q = 50  # vehicle capacity\n    vehicle_fixed_cost = 100.0\n    depot = [50, 50]\n    customers = [[39, 62], [95, 12], [17, 71], [38, 30], [26, 19]]\n    demands = [25, 20, 25, 17, 9]\n    time_windows = [[0, 307], [27, 119], [58, 121], [63, 134], [33, 107], [40, 95]]\n    service_times = [13, 5, 10, 15, 10]\n\n    # Node 0 = depot, nodes 1..N = customers\n    N = n_customers\n    nodes = list(range(N + 1))\n    cust_nodes = list(range(1, N + 1))\n\n    # Coordinates: index 0 = depot, 1..N = customers\n    coords = [depot] + customers\n\n    # COMPUTE distance matrix from coordinates\n    dist = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                dist[(i,j)] = math.sqrt(\n                    (coords[i][0]-coords[j][0])**2 +\n                    (coords[i][1]-coords[j][1])**2)\n\n    # Service time by node (depot = 0)\n    svc = [0] + service_times\n\n    # Big-M for time linking\n    T_max = time_windows[0][1]\n    max_svc = max(service_times)\n    max_dist = max(dist.values())\n    M = T_max + max_svc + max_dist + 1\n\n    # --- Variables ---\n    # Arc variables (binary)\n    x = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        for j in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                x[(i,j)] = m.addVar(vtype=GRB.BINARY, name=f\"Arc_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # Load variables (MTZ subtour elimination)\n    u = {}\n    for i in cust_nodes:\n        u[i] = m.addVar(lb=demands[i-1], ub=Q, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Load_{i}\")\n\n    # Time variables\n    t = {}\n    for i in nodes:\n        a, b = time_windows[i]\n        t[i] = m.addVar(lb=a, ub=b, vtype=GRB.CONTINUOUS, name=f\"Time_{i}\")\n    # Fix depot departure to time 0\n    t[0].ub = 0.0\n\n    # --- Objective ---\n    # Minimize travel distance + vehicle fixed costs\n    obj = quicksum(dist[(i,j)] * x[(i,j)] for i in nodes for j in nodes if i != j)\n    obj += quicksum(vehicle_fixed_cost * x[(0,j)] for j in cust_nodes)\n    m.setObjective(obj, GRB.MINIMIZE)\n\n    # --- Constraints ---\n    # 1. Each customer has exactly one outgoing arc\n    for i in cust_nodes:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for j in nodes if j != i) == 1,\n            name=f\"VisitOut_C{i}\")\n\n    # 2. Each customer has exactly one incoming arc\n    for j in cust_nodes:\n        m.addConstr(\n            quicksum(x[(i,j)] for i in nodes if i != j) == 1,\n            name=f\"VisitIn_C{j}\")\n\n    # 3. At most K vehicles leave the depot\n    m.addConstr(\n        quicksum(x[(0,j)] for j in cust_nodes) <= n_vehicles,\n        name=\"VehicleLimit\")\n\n    # 4. MTZ subtour elimination\n    for i in cust_nodes:\n        for j in cust_nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    u[i] - u[j] + Q * x[(i,j)] <= Q - demands[j-1],\n                    name=f\"MTZ_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    # 5. Time linking: if arc (i,j) is used, arrival at j >= departure from i\n    for j in cust_nodes:\n        for i in nodes:\n            if i != j:\n                m.addConstr(\n                    t[i] - t[j] + M * x[(i,j)] <= M - svc[i] - dist[(i,j)],\n                    name=f\"TimeLink_{i}_{j}\")\n\n    m.optimize()\n    return m\n```",
    "LLM_description": "Memo: Vehicle Routing and Scheduling Challenge\n\nOverview\nWe operate a fleet of identical delivery vehicles from a central depot to serve a defined set of customers, each with a promised delivery time window. For the upcoming planning horizon, all vehicles will depart the depot at time 0, make a single continuous trip to visit their assigned customers in sequence, and return to the depot before closing time as specified in the Annex. No vehicle will perform multiple runs.\n\nRouting and Scheduling Challenge\nThe core task is to determine:\n- Which customers each vehicle should serve, and\n- In what visiting order each vehicle should proceed,\nso that every customer is visited exactly once, all time commitments are met, and each vehicle returns to the depot before the end of the operating day. Travel time between any two locations is determined by the straight-line distance between their coordinates, assuming a unit speed, so one unit of distance equals one unit of time.\n\nOperating Requirements and Rules\n- Time windows and waiting: Each customer has an earliest and latest acceptable delivery time. Service must begin within this window. If a vehicle arrives early, it will wait until the window opens. After service is complete, the vehicle departs immediately. A vehicle cannot begin service after the latest acceptable time.\n- Service times: Each stop includes a defined unloading/service duration. A vehicle\u2019s departure time from a stop is the actual service start time plus the service duration.\n- Capacity limits: Vehicles are identical and carry limited capacity. The total demand assigned to any vehicle\u2019s route must not exceed its capacity at any point. No split deliveries are permitted; each customer is served by exactly one vehicle during this single visit.\n- Single trip per vehicle: Every vehicle leaves the depot at time 0, completes its assigned sequence of customer visits without returning mid-route, and must be back at the depot before the depot closing time stated in the Annex.\n- Costs: Using a vehicle incurs a fixed deployment cost. In addition, there is a distance-based travel cost. The plan should balance using more vehicles (incurring higher fixed costs) against longer routes (incurring more travel distance), while honoring time windows and capacity.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a set of vehicle routes and visit sequences that:\n- Serve every customer exactly once,\n- Respect all delivery time windows (with waiting allowed if early),\n- Observe vehicle capacity at all times,\n- Ensure all vehicles return to the depot before the planning horizon ends, and\n- Minimize the sum of total travel distance and the fixed cost for each vehicle used.\n\nDeliverable\nThe routing plan should specify, for each deployed vehicle, its customer sequence, expected arrival and departure times at each stop (including any waiting), and confirmation of capacity adherence, culminating in an on-time return to the depot. The overall plan should achieve the lowest feasible total cost under these rules.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Operations Data\n- Depot location (coordinates): (50, 50)\n- Depot closing time (latest return): 307\n- Customer locations (coordinates): Customer Locations (coordinates):\n  - C1: (39, 62)\n  - C2: (95, 12)\n  - C3: (17, 71)\n  - C4: (38, 30)\n  - C5: (26, 19)\n- Customer demands (units): Customer Demands:\n  - C1: 25 units\n  - C2: 20 units\n  - C3: 25 units\n  - C4: 17 units\n  - C5: 9 units\n- Customer time windows (earliest, latest): Customer Time Windows [earliest, latest]:\n  - C1: [27, 119]\n  - C2: [58, 121]\n  - C3: [63, 134]\n  - C4: [33, 107]\n  - C5: [40, 95]\n- Customer service times (time units): Service Times (minutes at customer):\n  - C1: 13\n  - C2: 5\n  - C3: 10\n  - C4: 15\n  - C5: 10\n- Vehicle capacity (units): 50\n- Number of vehicles available: 3\n- Fixed deployment cost per vehicle: 100.0",
    "LLM_description_template": "Memo: Vehicle Routing and Scheduling Challenge\n\nOverview\nWe operate a fleet of identical delivery vehicles from a central depot to serve a defined set of customers, each with a promised delivery time window. For the upcoming planning horizon, all vehicles will depart the depot at time 0, make a single continuous trip to visit their assigned customers in sequence, and return to the depot before closing time as specified in the Annex. No vehicle will perform multiple runs.\n\nRouting and Scheduling Challenge\nThe core task is to determine:\n- Which customers each vehicle should serve, and\n- In what visiting order each vehicle should proceed,\nso that every customer is visited exactly once, all time commitments are met, and each vehicle returns to the depot before the end of the operating day. Travel time between any two locations is determined by the straight-line distance between their coordinates, assuming a unit speed, so one unit of distance equals one unit of time.\n\nOperating Requirements and Rules\n- Time windows and waiting: Each customer has an earliest and latest acceptable delivery time. Service must begin within this window. If a vehicle arrives early, it will wait until the window opens. After service is complete, the vehicle departs immediately. A vehicle cannot begin service after the latest acceptable time.\n- Service times: Each stop includes a defined unloading/service duration. A vehicle\u2019s departure time from a stop is the actual service start time plus the service duration.\n- Capacity limits: Vehicles are identical and carry limited capacity. The total demand assigned to any vehicle\u2019s route must not exceed its capacity at any point. No split deliveries are permitted; each customer is served by exactly one vehicle during this single visit.\n- Single trip per vehicle: Every vehicle leaves the depot at time 0, completes its assigned sequence of customer visits without returning mid-route, and must be back at the depot before the depot closing time stated in the Annex.\n- Costs: Using a vehicle incurs a fixed deployment cost. In addition, there is a distance-based travel cost. The plan should balance using more vehicles (incurring higher fixed costs) against longer routes (incurring more travel distance), while honoring time windows and capacity.\n\nGoal\nDevelop a set of vehicle routes and visit sequences that:\n- Serve every customer exactly once,\n- Respect all delivery time windows (with waiting allowed if early),\n- Observe vehicle capacity at all times,\n- Ensure all vehicles return to the depot before the planning horizon ends, and\n- Minimize the sum of total travel distance and the fixed cost for each vehicle used.\n\nDeliverable\nThe routing plan should specify, for each deployed vehicle, its customer sequence, expected arrival and departure times at each stop (including any waiting), and confirmation of capacity adherence, culminating in an on-time return to the depot. The overall plan should achieve the lowest feasible total cost under these rules.\n\nAnnex \u2013 Operations Data\n- Depot location (coordinates): {DEPOT_LOCATION}\n- Depot closing time (latest return): {DEPOT_CLOSING_TIME}\n- Customer locations (coordinates): {CUSTOMER_LOCATIONS}\n- Customer demands (units): {CUSTOMER_DEMANDS}\n- Customer time windows (earliest, latest): {TIME_WINDOWS}\n- Customer service times (time units): {SERVICE_TIMES}\n- Vehicle capacity (units): {VEHICLE_CAPACITY}\n- Number of vehicles available: {NUM_VEHICLES}\n- Fixed deployment cost per vehicle: {VEHICLE_FIXED_COST}"
